{"name":"Pink Plastic Fantastic","hex":"#fc80c3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"},{"term":"plastic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A synthetic, solid, hydrocarbon-based polymer, whether thermoplastic or thermosetting.","Credit or debit cards used in place of cash to buy goods and services."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Capable of being moulded; malleable, flexible, pliant. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Producing tissue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plastic"},{"term":"fantastic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wonderful; marvelous; excellent; extraordinarily good or great (used especially as an intensifier).","Existing in or constructed from fantasy; of or relating to fantasy; fanciful."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fanciful or whimsical person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fantastic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plastic Fantastic","blocks":[{"text":"Plastic Fantastic: How the Biggest Fraud in Physics Shook the Scientific World is a 2009 book by U.S.-based science reporter Eugenie Samuel Reich."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/19/Plastic_Fantastic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plastic_Fantastic","wikidata":"Q7202048"},{"title":"Plastic","blocks":[{"text":"Plastics are a wide range of synthetic or semisynthetic materials composed primarily of polymers. Their defining characteristic, plasticity, allows them to be molded, extruded, or pressed into a diverse range of solid forms. This adaptability, combined with a wide range of other properties such as low weight, durability, flexibility, chemical resistance, low toxicity, and low-cost production, has led to their widespread use around the world. While most plastics are produced from natural gas and petroleum, a growing minority are produced from renewable resources like polylactic acid."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Plastic_household_items.jpg/330px-Plastic_household_items.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plastic","wikidata":"Q11474"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Melted_plastic.jpg/330px-Melted_plastic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Melted_plastic.jpg/330px-Melted_plastic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The plastic handle of a kitchen utensil, deformed by heat. This behavior, in which the plastic resolidifies in a new shape after melting, is characteristic of thermoplastics.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMelted_plastic.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Slovnaft_-_new_polypropylene_plant_PP3.JPG/500px-Slovnaft_-_new_polypropylene_plant_PP3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&